===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{term-label|en|humorous}}

# A [[fictional]] [[component]] or [[material#Noun|material]] which, if it [[exist]]ed, would [[enable]] one to [[easily]] [[solve]] a [[hard]] [[problem]].
#: {{synonyms|en|handwavium}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles N. Kelber|chapter=Research Reactors|editor=Earl W. Phelan|title=Current Trends in Nuclear Power: A Symposium Sponsored by the University of Arizona in Cooperation with Argonne National Laboratory: Tucson, Arizona, February 26&nbsp;– March 12, 1962|location=Argonne, Ill.|publisher={{w|Argonne National Laboratory}}|year=1962|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=GKQFthdri18C&pg=PA17|column=2|oclc=4732239|passage=Thus the invention of high temperature fuels will enable us to consider cores which are radiation cooled; such cores would be surrounded by large tanks (cooled by water) which in turn would be penetrated by beam holes. The beam holes themselves would probably have to be made of "'''unobtanium'''". (This "'''unobtanium'''" is a word coined at Atomics International to describe the material needed to make reactor projects feasible.) In between the radiation cooled reactors and the water cooled reactors is a regime suitable for liquid metal (including boiling liquid) cooled reactors. Once again "'''unobtanium'''" is needed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lynn C. Rogers|author2=Ahid D. Nashif|title=Computerized Processing and Empirical Processing of Viscoelastic Material Property Data and Preliminary Constrained Layer Damping Treatment Design|journal=The Shock and Vibration Bulletin|series=Bulletin|seriesvolume=48, part 2 (Isolation and Damping, Impact, Blast)|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:Shock and Vibration Information Analysis Center|The Shock and Vibration Information Center]], [[w:United States Naval Research Laboratory|Naval Research Laboratory]]|month=September|year=1978|page=26|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=i7IjAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA26|column=2|oclc=660876214|passage=In order to illustrate some of the features of data processing under ideal conditions, a set of simulated data was generated and is presented in the last four columns of Table II. The hypothetical material is called '''Unobtainium'''.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=T[homas] A. Heppenheimer|authorlink=Thomas A. Heppenheimer|chapter=A Shuttle to Fit the Budget|title=The Space Shuttle Decision: NASA’s Search for a Reusable Space Vehicle|series=The NASA History Series|seriesvolume=NASA SP-4221|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=Office of Policy and Plans, NASA History Office, [[w:NASA|National Aeronautics and Space Administration]]|year=1999|page=331|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vUk7AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA331|oclc=1109173431|passage=As early as 1969, during the initial Phase A studies, Lockheed had taken the initiative in proposing a two-stage fully-reusable design with both stages built of aluminum and using silica tiles for thermal protection. [...] Though that company indeed was in the forefront in developing such tiles, they were items for laboratory research. A design that specified their use had no more intrinsic credibility than one that proposed to use the miracle metals '''Unobtanium''' and Wishalloy.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Kevin Cameron|authorlink=Kevin Cameron (journalist)|title=Supercharged: It’s Good to B-King|magazine=Cycle World|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Hachette Filipacchi Media U.S.|Hachette Filipacchi Magazines]]|month=February|year=2002|volume=41|issue=2|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mt5qiI62GPYC&pg=RA2-PA38|column=2|issn=0011-4286|oclc=920424477|passage=This is not a Tom Tomorrow Bike, made of NASA '''unobtainiums''' and employing warp drives that have yet to be invented. It could be built as soon as the production technology is put on the line and powered-up, because all the vehicle technology is proven, off-the-shelf stuff.}}
# An especially [[rare]] component that is almost [[impossible]] to [[get hold of]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kevin Gosselin|chapter=27|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cMm6BAAAQBAJ&pg=PT123|title=Hunt for the Blower Bentley|location=London|publisher=MX Publishing|year=2011|isbn=978-1-78092-018-4|passage=It was this blend of genuine, authentic Bentley parts with his recreations of the '''unobtanium''' bits that had Patrick thinking that this ruse would actually work.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ellen Grabiner|chapter=Did You See That?|editors=Donald E. Palumbo and C. W. Sullivan III|title=I See You: The Shifting Paradigms of {{w|James Cameron}}’s [[w:Avatar (2009 film)|Avatar]]|series=Critical Explorations in Science Fiction and Fantasy|seriesvolume=34|location=Jefferson, N.C.|publisher={{w|McFarland & Company}}|year=2012|page=22|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=P49zr4QiBjQC&pg=PA22|isbn=978-0-7864-6492-0|passage=We might recognize, here, the blind pursuit of our own '''''unobtaniums''''' and the ways in which our ancestors forged ahead without care for the consequences of their actions to native peoples or to their environs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Joe Quirk|authorlink=Joe Quirk|author2=with {{w|Patri Friedman}}|chapter=Fuel: The Ocean is a Solar Panel|title=Seasteading: How Floating Nations will Restore the Environment, Enrich the Poor, Cure the Sick, and Liberate Humanity from Politicians|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Free Press (publisher)|Free Press]]|month=March|year=2017|section=part II (Environment)|page=159|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=MzhYDgAAQBAJ&pg=PA159|isbn=978-1-4516-9926-5|passage=These precious [rare earth] minerals are not actually rare, just so infernally difficult to sift from the earth without destroying the environment and poisoning your workers that the minerals are known in the mining industry as "'''unobtainiums'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Cory Doctorow|authorlink=Cory Doctorow|editor={{w|Patrick Nielsen Hayden}}|title=[[w:Walkaway (Doctorow novel)|Walkaway: A Novel]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Tor Books}}|month=April|year=2017|section=section v|pages=186–187|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=MKMsDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA187|isbn=978-0-7653-9276-3|passage=People who think about space end up thinking about bullshit like {{w|Star Wars}} and {{w|Star Trek}}. [...] They have to invent '''unobtaniums''', magic crystals that, for some reason, can't just be printed out by their transporter beams, or there's no story.}}
# A [[high technology]] component that is [[extremely]] [[expensive]].

